\DOC ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_BYTES128_TAC

\TYPE {ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_BYTES128_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves goals stating that two 128-bit byte-sized components at related
addresses are orthogonal, closing them outright.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is
{orthogonal_components t u}, where {t} and {u} are both 128-bit byte components
whose addresses share the same base, one of them possibly offset by a literal
word, it solves the goal:
{
   A ?- orthogonal_components t u
   ========================================  ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_BYTES128_TAC
}

\FAILURE
Fails when the goal's conclusion is not an orthogonality statement between two
components of this particular 128-bit byte form — for example when the
components have a different width or kind, or when the two addresses are built
from different base terms — the failure coming from an internal matching step
that finds no instance of the required shape.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this one kind of successful outcome appears, on
goals with no assumptions and with both component arguments built from the same
base address.

\ENDDOC
